[38;5;231m⏺[39m Settled: [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m with a [38;5;153mtrackLoginEvent[39m seam.

  Last question that changes the shape of things, then I'll put the design in front of you.

  [1mThe fork:[0m which events get recorded. This matters now rather than later because it decides whether [38;5;153muserId[39m is always
  present in an event or sometimes absent — and that's the field other code will come to depend on.

  - [1mSuccessful logins only[0m — one call, after login returns success. Every event has a real [38;5;153muserId[39m. Buys the simplest
    possible event shape and matches what you asked for literally. Costs: adding failures later means [38;5;153muserId[39m becomes
    nullable, and anything reading events has to handle that.
  - [1mSuccesses and failed authentication[0m — events for both, with [38;5;153muserId[39m null on failure plus a [38;5;153msuccess[39m boolean. Buys the
    security-relevant half of login tracking (repeated failures against one username). Costs: nullable [38;5;153muserId[39m from day
    one, and a fabricated failure path, since the stub always returns success.
  - [1mEverything, including client-side validation errors[0m — also records the "Missing required fields" case at [38;5;153mapp.js:11[39m.
    Buys funnel visibility. Costs: mixes two different kinds of event into one stream; those aren't login attempts,
    they're form errors.

  [1mMy recommendation: successful logins only.[0m You said "track who logged in," and that's what this is. The stub always
  returns [38;5;153m{ success: true }[39m, so a failure path today would be untestable theater. The seam makes adding failures later a
  change in one file — and I'd rather you decide to add them when there's a real auth backend to produce real failures
  than pay for a nullable field now.
